00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
Email. that is folks.

316
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:00,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

317
00:14:08,000 --> 00:14:10,000
In podcast number 65,

318
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:12,000
I turn the tables on you

319
00:14:12,000 --> 00:14:14,000
and I asked you four different questions

320
00:14:14,000 --> 00:14:17,000
And I received a whole bunch of answers.

321
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
I've weeded through them a little bit

322
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
and I'm going to give you some

323
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:24,000
ones that I selected that sounded really good

324
00:14:24,000 --> 00:14:27,000
And I'm going to answer three of those questions.

325
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
One of the questions was about

326
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
high adventure opportunities

327
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:34,000
in the central part of the United States,

328
00:14:34,000 --> 00:14:37,000
And those answers are going to show up

329
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,000
online as posts in the

330
00:14:40,000 --> 00:14:42,000
High Adventure Resources area

331
00:14:42,000 --> 00:14:44,000
of ScoutsMasterCGcom,

332
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:47,000
So be looking for those there.

333
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
And we're going to answer the other three questions

334
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
right here right now.

335
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
The first one that we came up with

336
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
was: what do you do when a ScoutsMaster is retiring

337
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
but he doesn't really leave

338
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
And you know he's causing problems

339
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:02,000
and he's interfering with the present ScoutsMaster?

340
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:04,000
There were two basic

341
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:06,000
strings of thought in most of the answers

342
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:08,000
I received on this.

343
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:10,000
One was the nice guy way

344
00:15:10,000 --> 00:15:13,000
and one was the old warhorse way.

345
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:19,000
Now, I think both of them are perfectly valid,

346
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
and I'm going to start with Larry Geiger,

347
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
who's in Cocoa Beach, Florida.

348
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
There's a ScoutsMaster there

349
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
And this is his second go round as a ScoutsMaster.

350
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
Larry was a ScoutsMaster for another troop

351
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
in another place

352
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
and then became a ScoutsMaster again.

353
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
And Larry has this to say:

354
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
He says: you really only have two options,

355
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
and the first one is to get rid of them.

356
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:42,000
And Larry, I wholeheartedly agree.

357
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:44,000
Larry's an old warhorse, and so am I,

358
00:15:44,000 --> 00:15:46,000
In the best sense of the term, I hope

359
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:48,000
In the best sense of the term.

360
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:50,000
You want to be as nice as possible,

361
00:15:50,000 --> 00:15:52,000
You want to be kind and considerate

362
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:54,000
and a good person,

363
00:15:54,000 --> 00:15:56,000
But sometimes

364
00:15:56,000 --> 00:15:59,000
volunteers need to be fired.

365
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
I'm saying it.

366
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:02,000
I am saying it.

367
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Sometimes they need to go,

368
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:06,000
And to do that, you get the

369
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:08,000
Chartered Organization representative

370
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:10,000
and the committee chairman

371
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
and you sit down and you talk

372
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:14,000
and you make a move

373
00:16:14,000 --> 00:16:16,000
and you get it done

374
00:16:16,000 --> 00:16:18,000
and you get rid of the problem.

375
00:16:18,000 --> 00:16:20,000
Sometimes that's the only solution.

376
00:16:20,000 --> 00:16:22,000
It's extreme,

377
00:16:22,000 --> 00:16:24,000
but I'm sharing it with you because

378
00:16:24,000 --> 00:16:26,000
if you are presently finding yourself

379
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:28,000
in this situation,

380
00:16:28,000 --> 00:16:30,000
that may be your alternative

381
00:16:30,000 --> 00:16:32,000
and that may be what you need to do,

382
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
Without going into a broad

383
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
explanation and detail

384
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:38,000
of exactly how this process

385
00:16:38,000 --> 00:16:40,000
happens and

386
00:16:40,000 --> 00:16:42,000
the different issues involved in it.

387
00:16:42,000 --> 00:16:44,000
let's just know

388
00:16:44,000 --> 00:16:46,000
that that is one alternative.

389
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:48,000
If you need

390
00:16:48,000 --> 00:16:50,000
some help with a situation like this,

391
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:52,000
get in touch.

392
00:16:52,000 --> 00:16:54,000
Do what I can.

393
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:56,000
The second one was:

394
00:16:56,000 --> 00:16:58,000
I'll go to Larry's second answer

395
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
because it was backed up

396
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
by several others.

397
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:04,000
Larry said work with the person

398
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:06,000
and he pointed this out.

399
00:17:06,000 --> 00:17:08,000
he says a troop can only have

400
00:17:08,000 --> 00:17:10,000
one scout master and only one.

401
00:17:10,000 --> 00:17:12,000
I can imagine two committee chairs,

402
00:17:12,000 --> 00:17:14,000
maybe splitting up some of the responsibility

403
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,000
of the leaders.

404
00:17:16,000 --> 00:17:18,000
The scouts,

405
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
the boy leaders

406
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
and the other adults involved with the troop

407
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,000
have to know who has the last word on some things

408
00:17:24,000 --> 00:17:26,000
and you need one scout master.

409
00:17:26,000 --> 00:17:28,000
Larry goes on to say this.

410
00:17:28,000 --> 00:17:30,000
he says: when I retired

411
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:32,000
from being scout master of my first troop,

412
00:17:32,000 --> 00:17:34,000
I joined the committee.

413
00:17:34,000 --> 00:17:36,000
I helped out a little with Eagles

414
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:38,000
and I went on a couple

415
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
of backpacking trips.

416
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:42,000
they went on a 50 miler every other year

417
00:17:42,000 --> 00:17:44,000
with that for about six years.

418
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:46,000
but that's about it

419
00:17:46,000 --> 00:17:48,000
and in retrospect that might have been too much.

420
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:50,000
Larry found a place.

421
00:17:50,000 --> 00:17:52,000
having been the scout master before,

422
00:17:52,000 --> 00:17:54,000
having understood the program,

423
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:56,000
found a way to get out of the way. but still help.

424
00:17:56,000 --> 00:17:58,000
Steve Janssen,

425
00:17:58,000 --> 00:18:00,000
he's a scout master too,

426
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:02,000
from troop 228 in Santa Clarita, California.

427
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:04,000
Steve was

428
00:18:04,000 --> 00:18:06,000
on the nice guy side of things

429
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:08,000
but

430
00:18:08,000 --> 00:18:10,000
he still understood that this could be a problem

431
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:12,000
and he suggested

432
00:18:12,000 --> 00:18:14,000
the same thing that Larry did.

433
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:16,000
there may be another position,

434
00:18:16,000 --> 00:18:18,000
there may be a way for them to still

435
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:20,000
remain and help out the troop.

436
00:18:20,000 --> 00:18:22,000
there's also Bill McFarlane

437
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:24,000
who is up in Pittsfield, Massachusetts.

438
00:18:24,000 --> 00:18:26,000
scout master

439
00:18:26,000 --> 00:18:28,000
troop 8 suggested that

440
00:18:28,000 --> 00:18:30,000
unexperienced scout master would be a valuable person

441
00:18:30,000 --> 00:18:32,000
to have at the district level as a commissioner.

442
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
but going back to what Steve had to say,

443
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:36,000
he said I think it's good to have

444
00:18:36,000 --> 00:18:38,000
the person as a mentor,

445
00:18:38,000 --> 00:18:40,000
assertive,

446
00:18:40,000 --> 00:18:42,000
listen to their advice and say: well,

447
00:18:42,000 --> 00:18:44,000
here's what I'm going to do. anyway,

448
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:46,000
I'm not really asking for your approval

449
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:48,000
and I'm going to move forward.

450
00:18:48,000 --> 00:18:50,000
and the old scout master needs

451
00:18:50,000 --> 00:18:52,000
to understand that

452
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:54,000
he may see his job as filling a vacuum

453
00:18:54,000 --> 00:18:56,000
and

454
00:18:56,000 --> 00:18:58,000
if you don't let that vacuum exist in the first place,

455
00:18:58,000 --> 00:19:00,000
you won't have the problem.

456
00:19:00,000 --> 00:19:02,000
I think those are some pretty good answers

457
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:04,000
that should help define a couple of alternatives

458
00:19:04,000 --> 00:19:06,000
and just to review

459
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,000
one is: hey, sometimes

460
00:19:08,000 --> 00:19:10,000
you got a clean house

461
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:12,000
and you got to get

462
00:19:12,000 --> 00:19:14,000
an interfering volunteer out of the way,

463
00:19:14,000 --> 00:19:16,000
and there's ways to make that happen.

464
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
that's one alternative.

465
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
the second one is making sure that they have a responsibility,

466
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:22,000
that it's

467
00:19:22,000 --> 00:19:24,000
clearly understood that that's their responsibility

468
00:19:24,000 --> 00:19:26,000
and that they have

469
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:28,000
retired from other responsibilities

470
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,000
and being assertive about it.

471
00:19:30,000 --> 00:19:32,000
one of the ways Steve

472
00:19:32,000 --> 00:19:34,000
puts it is:

473
00:19:34,000 --> 00:19:36,000
you need to let them know

474
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:38,000
that they made a real difference

475
00:19:38,000 --> 00:19:40,000
and their experience is still valued,

476
00:19:40,000 --> 00:19:42,000
and then to use their advice

477
00:19:42,000 --> 00:19:44,000
and their counsel,

478
00:19:44,000 --> 00:19:46,000
but to make it clear that it's now your responsibility.

479
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:48,000
thanks, guys, for helping out with that.

480
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:50,000
the second question:

481
00:19:50,000 --> 00:19:52,000
I'm not going to tell you where the answers come from,

482
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:54,000
and the reason is it's a little sensitive.

483
00:19:54,000 --> 00:19:56,000
the second question is:

484
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:58,000
how do you address a scout

485
00:19:58,000 --> 00:20:00,000
who gets in trouble outside of scouts?

486
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,000
here's a couple possible scenarios:

487
00:20:02,000 --> 00:20:04,000
he gets stopped by the police because he's involved

488
00:20:04,000 --> 00:20:06,000
in some kind of a physical altercation

489
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:08,000
and maybe charges

490
00:20:08,000 --> 00:20:10,000
stem from that.

491
00:20:10,000 --> 00:20:12,000
maybe he is

492
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:14,000
gets a traffic violation.

493
00:20:14,000 --> 00:20:16,000
maybe he has some kind of weapons violation.

494
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:18,000
maybe he gets in trouble at school

495
00:20:18,000 --> 00:20:20,000
for any one of a hundred reasons

496
00:20:20,000 --> 00:20:22,000
and ends up getting himself

497
00:20:22,000 --> 00:20:24,000
suspended or even expelled.

498
00:20:24,000 --> 00:20:26,000
what kind of notice does the troop

499
00:20:26,000 --> 00:20:28,000
need to take of it?

500
00:20:28,000 --> 00:20:30,000
how do you deal with it

501
00:20:30,000 --> 00:20:32,000
and what happens

502
00:20:32,000 --> 00:20:34,000
to the scout?

503
00:20:34,000 --> 00:20:36,000
everyone who answered the question agreed that

504
00:20:36,000 --> 00:20:38,000
there's no universal way to handle this.

505
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:40,000
it all depends on what the incident was

506
00:20:40,000 --> 00:20:42,000
and that the decision point

507
00:20:42,000 --> 00:20:44,000
came down to: is this

508
00:20:44,000 --> 00:20:46,000
a clear and present danger

509
00:20:46,000 --> 00:20:48,000
to any other scouts?

510
00:20:48,000 --> 00:20:50,000
so if a boy gets a traffic violation

511
00:20:50,000 --> 00:20:52,000
for speeding,

512
00:20:52,000 --> 00:20:54,000
not necessarily a clear and present danger

513
00:20:54,000 --> 00:20:56,000
to any other scouts,

514
00:20:56,000 --> 00:20:58,000
if he's having trouble at school,

515
00:20:58,000 --> 00:21:00,000
getting suspended for grades or something like that,

516
00:21:00,000 --> 00:21:02,000
you know,

517
00:21:02,000 --> 00:21:04,000
and that's one thing.

518
00:21:04,000 --> 00:21:06,000
if it's a violent problem,

519
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:08,000
if it's a weapons violation,

520
00:21:08,000 --> 00:21:10,000
if it's a drug violation,

521
00:21:10,000 --> 00:21:12,000
there was a pretty much agreement

522
00:21:12,000 --> 00:21:14,000
that this has to be looked at very seriously,

523
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:16,000
that it might involve

524
00:21:16,000 --> 00:21:18,000
asking the scout

525
00:21:18,000 --> 00:21:20,000
to step away from scouting

526
00:21:20,000 --> 00:21:22,000
for a period of time.

527
00:21:22,000 --> 00:21:24,000
I'm going to give you advice based on my experience

528
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:26,000
and based on the answers I got,

529
00:21:26,000 --> 00:21:28,000
and that is

530
00:21:28,000 --> 00:21:30,000
is that none of us-

531
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:32,000
unless you're a professional

532
00:21:32,000 --> 00:21:34,000
and you're volunteering as a scout leader,

533
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:36,000
none of us are law enforcement officers

534
00:21:36,000 --> 00:21:38,000
or lawyers or judges,

535
00:21:38,000 --> 00:21:40,000
and we have an excellent law

536
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:42,000
enforcement system,

537
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:44,000
we have a great court system

538
00:21:44,000 --> 00:21:46,000
and we have ways for these things to get sorted out,

539
00:21:46,000 --> 00:21:48,000
and

540
00:21:48,000 --> 00:21:50,000
I think it's very important,

541
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:52,000
before we make

542
00:21:52,000 --> 00:21:54,000
any long-term decisions,

543
00:21:54,000 --> 00:21:56,000
to let these things come to resolution.

544
00:21:56,000 --> 00:21:58,000
I did have a scout

545
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:00,000
who had a weapons violation

546
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:02,000
and it resulted

547
00:22:02,000 --> 00:22:04,000
in some charges.

548
00:22:04,000 --> 00:22:06,000
it resulted in a real mess for him.

549
00:22:06,000 --> 00:22:08,000
it was a very bad scene,

550
00:22:08,000 --> 00:22:10,000
but realizing that I'm not a professional

551
00:22:10,000 --> 00:22:12,000
investigator, policeman,

552
00:22:12,000 --> 00:22:14,000
I'm not a lawyer,

553
00:22:14,000 --> 00:22:16,000
I'm not a professional law

554
00:22:16,000 --> 00:22:18,000
enforcement person,

555
00:22:18,000 --> 00:22:20,000
what we had to decide to do was

556
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,000
to let the processes outside of scouting

557
00:22:22,000 --> 00:22:24,000
come to a place

558
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:26,000
and come to a conclusion

559
00:22:26,000 --> 00:22:28,000
before we were going to take any long-term action,

560
00:22:28,000 --> 00:22:30,000
since

561
00:22:30,000 --> 00:22:32,000
the charges

562
00:22:32,000 --> 00:22:34,000
were a clear and present danger to other scouts.

563
00:22:34,000 --> 00:22:36,000
if they proved to be true,

564
00:22:36,000 --> 00:22:38,000
we had to get in touch with his folks

565
00:22:38,000 --> 00:22:40,000
and say: look,

566
00:22:40,000 --> 00:22:42,000
the nature of the charges are such

567
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:44,000
that we're going to have to ask him to step away

568
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:46,000
until they're resolved.

569
00:22:46,000 --> 00:22:48,000
this isn't a punishment,

570
00:22:48,000 --> 00:22:50,000
we're not trying to

571
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:52,000
be even more miserable,

572
00:22:52,000 --> 00:22:54,000
but it's our duty

573
00:22:54,000 --> 00:22:56,000
to our scouts and families

574
00:22:56,000 --> 00:22:58,000
to take this step.

575
00:22:58,000 --> 00:23:00,000
it was not a happy day for me,

576
00:23:00,000 --> 00:23:02,000
not a happy day at all,

577
00:23:02,000 --> 00:23:04,000
but

578
00:23:04,000 --> 00:23:06,000
that's unfortunately what has to happen

579
00:23:06,000 --> 00:23:08,000
sometimes.

580
00:23:08,000 --> 00:23:10,000
I do want to give credit for this answer to Steve Jensen,

581
00:23:10,000 --> 00:23:12,000
because

582
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:14,000
I think it's just general advice

583
00:23:14,000 --> 00:23:16,000
and Steve wasn't relating

584
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,000
to me any specific

585
00:23:18,000 --> 00:23:20,000
or anything like that.

586
00:23:20,000 --> 00:23:22,000
but he said: at this point

587
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:24,000
the boy's entire world is likely to have crashed around him,

588
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:26,000
he's suspended from school

589
00:23:26,000 --> 00:23:28,000
or he's in trouble and his family is likely very angry

590
00:23:28,000 --> 00:23:30,000
and may be punishing him too,

591
00:23:30,000 --> 00:23:32,000
and the troop doesn't necessarily have to join in.

592
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:36,000
we need to remind him about the responsibilities

593
00:23:36,000 --> 00:23:38,000
he accepts each time he states

594
00:23:38,000 --> 00:23:40,000
the scout oath of law.

595
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:42,000
we need to tell him

596
00:23:42,000 --> 00:23:44,000
we expect him to meet those responsibilities

597
00:23:44,000 --> 00:23:46,000
and then we need to believe in him.

598
00:23:46,000 --> 00:23:48,000
this is probably a critical juncture

599
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:50,000
in that boy's life.

600
00:23:50,000 --> 00:23:52,000
which direction is he going to go?

601
00:23:52,000 --> 00:23:54,000
is he going to continue on a downward spiral

602
00:23:54,000 --> 00:23:56,000
or is he going to get back on track?

603
00:23:56,000 --> 00:23:58,000
there's likely enough punishment being doled out

604
00:23:58,000 --> 00:24:00,000
to convince the boy he's done something wrong.

605
00:24:00,000 --> 00:24:02,000
the troop

606
00:24:02,000 --> 00:24:04,000
could be a safe haven,

607
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:06,000
a place where people believe in him

608
00:24:06,000 --> 00:24:08,000
and just want him to do better.

609
00:24:08,000 --> 00:24:10,000
Steve, that's excellently said

610
00:24:10,000 --> 00:24:12,000
and I wanted to make sure you were tied to that statement.

611
00:24:12,000 --> 00:24:14,000
that's just

612
00:24:14,000 --> 00:24:16,000
really really good advice,

613
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,000
and there are any number of incidents

614
00:24:18,000 --> 00:24:20,000
where this is going to be the advice you want to take,

615
00:24:22,000 --> 00:24:24,000
where you want to back the child up.

616
00:24:24,000 --> 00:24:26,000
he is a child.

617
00:24:26,000 --> 00:24:28,000
we do have a juvenile justice system

618
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:30,000
that is different from the adult justice system

619
00:24:30,000 --> 00:24:32,000
because it understands that there are different levels

620
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,000
of responsibility

621
00:24:34,000 --> 00:24:36,000
in people in their developmental years.

622
00:24:36,000 --> 00:24:38,000
I had a response

623
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,000
from another scout master.

624
00:24:40,000 --> 00:24:42,000
he said some time ago a scout from my troop

625
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:44,000
was at school

626
00:24:44,000 --> 00:24:46,000
after hours with a group of students

627
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,000
and he got in trouble.

628
00:24:48,000 --> 00:24:50,000
the school handled the issue.

629
00:24:50,000 --> 00:24:52,000
it came to my attention

630
00:24:52,000 --> 00:24:54,000
and he and I sat down and discussed it.

631
00:24:54,000 --> 00:24:56,000
since the school did not press charges

632
00:24:56,000 --> 00:24:58,000
and the scout

633
00:24:58,000 --> 00:25:00,000
abided by the school's decision regarding punishment,

634
00:25:00,000 --> 00:25:02,000
I felt that there wasn't really any need

635
00:25:02,000 --> 00:25:04,000
for us to take further action

636
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:06,000
than the discussion the scout and I had

637
00:25:06,000 --> 00:25:08,000
as to what to tell the parents of the other scouts.

638
00:25:08,000 --> 00:25:10,000
I directed them to contact

639
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:12,000
the scout directly

640
00:25:12,000 --> 00:25:14,000
if they wanted any more information.

641
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,000
so that was a very good way of handling that.

642
00:25:16,000 --> 00:25:18,000
here's what you did.

643
00:25:18,000 --> 00:25:20,000
tell me about it.

644
00:25:20,000 --> 00:25:22,000
do you think the punishment that you received is fair?

645
00:25:22,000 --> 00:25:24,000
okay,

646
00:25:24,000 --> 00:25:26,000
so now we're both on board.

647
00:25:26,000 --> 00:25:28,000
we both understand what happened.

648
00:25:28,000 --> 00:25:30,000
how are we going to take care of this in the future?

649
00:25:30,000 --> 00:25:32,000
can you be trusted?

650
00:25:32,000 --> 00:25:34,000
are we going to have any problems

651
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,000
within the confines of a scout troop?

652
00:25:36,000 --> 00:25:38,000
the boys are going to talk to the boys

653
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:40,000
and the parents are going to find out

654
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:42,000
and they're going to want to know what's going on.

655
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:44,000
well,

656
00:25:44,000 --> 00:25:46,000
if you really want to know what's going on,

657
00:25:46,000 --> 00:25:48,000
go ahead and call their parents.

658
00:25:48,000 --> 00:25:50,000
I'm not going to tell you anything.

659
00:25:50,000 --> 00:25:52,000
I talked to the boy.

660
00:25:52,000 --> 00:25:54,000
I'm sure that there's no present danger to your son

661
00:25:54,000 --> 00:25:56,000
and if you think you need to know

662
00:25:56,000 --> 00:25:58,000
the details of this,

663
00:25:58,000 --> 00:26:00,000
call the boys parents.

664
00:26:00,000 --> 00:26:02,000
I think that's excellent advice.

665
00:26:02,000 --> 00:26:04,000
what do we tell parents? well, we tell the parents

666
00:26:04,000 --> 00:26:06,000
that we're on the scout's side,

667
00:26:06,000 --> 00:26:08,000
we're on his life and we need to be there for them.

668
00:26:08,000 --> 00:26:10,000
we're not going to send him away,

669
00:26:10,000 --> 00:26:12,000
that it's not a health and safety issue

670
00:26:12,000 --> 00:26:14,000
and we're not just shrugging it off,

671
00:26:14,000 --> 00:26:16,000
as boys will be boys,

672
00:26:16,000 --> 00:26:18,000
or that there's no big deal.

673
00:26:18,000 --> 00:26:20,000
but we feel that it's important for somebody

674
00:26:20,000 --> 00:26:22,000
to stand behind him and we're going to stand behind him.

675
00:26:22,000 --> 00:26:24,000
he knows he made a mistake

676
00:26:24,000 --> 00:26:26,000
and it doesn't necessarily mean it's the end of everything

677
00:26:26,000 --> 00:26:28,000
in his life.

678
00:26:28,000 --> 00:26:30,000
so some very good answers to that from several people

679
00:26:30,000 --> 00:26:32,000
and, like I said,

680
00:26:32,000 --> 00:26:34,000
I'm going to keep most of that

681
00:26:34,000 --> 00:26:36,000
just because of the sensitivity of the issue,

682
00:26:36,000 --> 00:26:38,000
as I will with

683
00:26:38,000 --> 00:26:40,000
another kind of sensitive issue,

684
00:26:40,000 --> 00:26:42,000
and that issue is

685
00:26:42,000 --> 00:26:44,000
what happens when you have a little flame war

686
00:26:44,000 --> 00:26:46,000
in your troop

687
00:26:46,000 --> 00:26:48,000
over the email.

688
00:26:48,000 --> 00:26:50,000
yes, the email,

689
00:26:50,000 --> 00:26:52,000
email is a wonderful thing until people start arguing

690
00:26:52,000 --> 00:26:54,000
back and forth on it.

691
00:26:54,000 --> 00:26:56,000
and people argue over email

692
00:26:56,000 --> 00:26:58,000
with a great deal more vitriol

693
00:26:58,000 --> 00:27:00,000
than they would ever argue in person.

694
00:27:00,000 --> 00:27:02,000
I can't associate some of the people who answered

695
00:27:02,000 --> 00:27:04,000
some of their answers

696
00:27:04,000 --> 00:27:06,000
because they're just good general answers.

697
00:27:06,000 --> 00:27:08,000
Larry Geiger said

698
00:27:08,000 --> 00:27:10,000
you really have to try and ignore

699
00:27:10,000 --> 00:27:12,000
people who do this.

700
00:27:12,000 --> 00:27:14,000
the best way to avoid it

701
00:27:14,000 --> 00:27:16,000
is to run all communication through one account

702
00:27:16,000 --> 00:27:18,000
with a blind distribution list.

703
00:27:18,000 --> 00:27:20,000
that way, if the reply comes back,

704
00:27:20,000 --> 00:27:22,000
the troop leadership can deal with it.

705
00:27:22,000 --> 00:27:24,000
by all means try to restrict having things distributed

706
00:27:24,000 --> 00:27:26,000
to someone

707
00:27:26,000 --> 00:27:28,000
so that they can just hit reply to all

708
00:27:28,000 --> 00:27:30,000
and start a flame war.

709
00:27:30,000 --> 00:27:32,000
the way that your emails are set up

710
00:27:32,000 --> 00:27:34,000
is important.

711
00:27:34,000 --> 00:27:36,000
if you don't understand the technicality of that,

712
00:27:36,000 --> 00:27:38,000
one of the boys in the troop

713
00:27:38,000 --> 00:27:40,000
probably does,

714
00:27:42,000 --> 00:27:44,000
or one of the adults involved

715
00:27:44,000 --> 00:27:46,000
with the committee will be able to get that set up.

716
00:27:46,000 --> 00:27:48,000
but yeah, sending out

717
00:27:48,000 --> 00:27:50,000
to a general distribution list

718
00:27:50,000 --> 00:27:52,000
in which all the emails are

719
00:27:52,000 --> 00:27:54,000
included

720
00:27:54,000 --> 00:27:56,000
in the sent to part

721
00:27:56,000 --> 00:27:58,000
of the email, very bad idea.

722
00:27:58,000 --> 00:28:00,000
very bad idea because now

723
00:28:00,000 --> 00:28:02,000
everybody who received the email

724
00:28:02,000 --> 00:28:04,000
can just hit reply all

725
00:28:04,000 --> 00:28:06,000
and then

726
00:28:06,000 --> 00:28:08,000
the cat's out of the bag and

727
00:28:08,000 --> 00:28:10,000
boy oh boy.

728
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:12,000
then you have a real mess.

729
00:28:12,000 --> 00:28:14,000
anonymous

730
00:28:14,000 --> 00:28:16,000
responder said this. I've had a lot of experience

731
00:28:16,000 --> 00:28:18,000
with this one. unfortunately,

732
00:28:18,000 --> 00:28:20,000
our chartered organization

733
00:28:20,000 --> 00:28:22,000
recently put a stop

734
00:28:22,000 --> 00:28:24,000
to a flame war

735
00:28:24,000 --> 00:28:26,000
by just

736
00:28:26,000 --> 00:28:28,000
going back and answering

737
00:28:28,000 --> 00:28:30,000
the original question

738
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,000
and laid out the basic principles.

739
00:28:32,000 --> 00:28:34,000
here's why we're here

740
00:28:34,000 --> 00:28:36,000
and based on that,

741
00:28:36,000 --> 00:28:38,000
here is the answer.

742
00:28:38,000 --> 00:28:40,000
he did it in a way that wasn't preachy,

743
00:28:40,000 --> 00:28:42,000
scolding or condescending,

744
00:28:42,000 --> 00:28:44,000
just a statement of fact.

745
00:28:44,000 --> 00:28:46,000
he didn't include the other emails in the chain.

746
00:28:46,000 --> 00:28:48,000
he didn't address any of the sniping

747
00:28:48,000 --> 00:28:50,000
or the other pettiness. they expressed

748
00:28:50,000 --> 00:28:52,000
just simple, calm,

749
00:28:52,000 --> 00:28:54,000
quiet, dignified

750
00:28:54,000 --> 00:28:56,000
responses.

751
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:58,000
that ended the discussion

752
00:28:58,000 --> 00:29:00,000
and I think everyone else went from being angry

753
00:29:00,000 --> 00:29:02,000
to being ashamed as they realized just how petty

754
00:29:02,000 --> 00:29:04,000
and silly they were being.

755
00:29:04,000 --> 00:29:06,000
Bill McFarland- he answered one of the other questions

756
00:29:06,000 --> 00:29:08,000
before. he said: my best suggestion is to

757
00:29:08,000 --> 00:29:10,000
have a sit down meeting,

758
00:29:10,000 --> 00:29:12,000
having anybody who's been

759
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:14,000
involved with the online

760
00:29:14,000 --> 00:29:16,000
mess sit down

761
00:29:16,000 --> 00:29:18,000
and talk. I definitely agree with Bill there

762
00:29:18,000 --> 00:29:20,000
that any emails you send out

763
00:29:20,000 --> 00:29:22,000
to your troop are informational,

764
00:29:22,000 --> 00:29:24,000
not editorial. informational,

765
00:29:24,000 --> 00:29:26,000
not editorial. you get the difference there.

766
00:29:26,000 --> 00:29:28,000
any comment on people's actions

767
00:29:28,000 --> 00:29:30,000
or thoughts, or especially

768
00:29:30,000 --> 00:29:32,000
if that comment can

769
00:29:32,000 --> 00:29:34,000
be read in any way

770
00:29:34,000 --> 00:29:36,000
to be a criticism- if

771
00:29:36,000 --> 00:29:38,000
someone needs to be criticized

772
00:29:38,000 --> 00:29:40,000
or otherwise needs their actions

773
00:29:40,000 --> 00:29:42,000
or words questioned, that needs

774
00:29:42,000 --> 00:29:44,000
to be done in person,

775
00:29:44,000 --> 00:29:46,000
not by an email.

776
00:29:46,000 --> 00:29:48,000
let's go back over the whole email biz.

777
00:29:48,000 --> 00:29:50,000
first of all, you can avoid

778
00:29:50,000 --> 00:29:52,000
the flame wars

779
00:29:52,000 --> 00:29:54,000
in two different ways.

780
00:29:54,000 --> 00:29:56,000
one is to assure that the way

781
00:29:56,000 --> 00:29:58,000
you're distributing emails doesn't allow

782
00:29:58,000 --> 00:30:00,000
people to reply to all

783
00:30:00,000 --> 00:30:02,000
and just send it back to the list,

784
00:30:02,000 --> 00:30:04,000
and that's very, very important.

785
00:30:04,000 --> 00:30:06,000
second way to avoid it

786
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:08,000
is to make sure

787
00:30:08,000 --> 00:30:10,000
that emails are informational,

788
00:30:10,000 --> 00:30:12,000
not editorial,

789
00:30:12,000 --> 00:30:14,000
and that if there

790
00:30:14,000 --> 00:30:16,000
is a problem that needs to be resolved,

791
00:30:16,000 --> 00:30:18,000
it's never addressed in an email.

792
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:20,000
it's done in person.

793
00:30:20,000 --> 00:30:22,000
how do you kill a flame war once it begins?

794
00:30:22,000 --> 00:30:24,000
well, a calm, dignified, wise response

795
00:30:24,000 --> 00:30:26,000
from somebody in authority,

796
00:30:26,000 --> 00:30:28,000
basically telling the children

797
00:30:28,000 --> 00:30:30,000
that are involved in the flame war

798
00:30:30,000 --> 00:30:32,000
to stop it. the other way is to take

799
00:30:32,000 --> 00:30:34,000
whatever the conflict is

800
00:30:34,000 --> 00:30:36,000
and turn it into a

801
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,000
face to face meeting.

802
00:30:38,000 --> 00:30:40,000
great advice. I knew you guys would come through

803
00:30:40,000 --> 00:30:42,000
and you did,

804
00:30:42,000 --> 00:30:44,000
and we really appreciate it.

805
00:30:44,000 --> 00:30:46,000
so that's three of the questions answered.

806
00:30:46,000 --> 00:30:48,000
thank you very much,

807
00:30:48,000 --> 00:30:50,000
and keep an eye on

808
00:30:50,000 --> 00:30:52,000
the high adventure section

809
00:30:52,000 --> 00:30:54,000
of the site and you will see

810
00:30:54,000 --> 00:30:56,000
some suggestions coming through

811
00:30:56,000 --> 00:30:58,000
for high adventure

812
00:30:58,000 --> 00:31:00,000
in the central part of the united states.

